2. This work
------------

    Copyright (c) 2026 Vin Gonzales
    Licensed under the GNU Affero General Public License v3.0 — see LICENSE.

Everything that makes this a web application rather than a desktop one was
written here and exists nowhere upstream. Upstream has no HTTP layer at all.
That includes, and is not limited to:

  - The HTTP API: /calculate, /calculate/status, /calculate/incoming,
    /calculate/breakpoints, /data/*, /share/:id, and the request and response
    shapes they speak.
  - The React front end in open-ps-calc-frontend/ in its entirety.
  - Breakpoints: the ASPD/cast/HIT threshold solver and its presentation.
  - Build comparison and pinning.
  - Share links, saved builds, and the Z3 share-key encoding.
  - Importing builds from the jaludev calculator, and skill trees from the
    Payon Stories planner.
  - The survivability / incoming-damage model.
  - Wildcard card mixing.
  - The Payon Stories server profile: the custom skills, reworked formulas,
    item and monster data, and the mechanic flags that separate PS from vanilla
    pre-renewal.
  - The test suite, the data audits, and the deployment pipeline.

Why AGPL rather than MIT: this is software people reach over a network and
never download. A licence whose conditions attach only to distributing copies
would leave the case this project actually faces — someone running a modified
copy as a public website — entirely unaddressed. The AGPL's section 13 is the
clause that covers it.

In plain terms, and the licence text governs where this summary is loose: you
may use, study, change and redistribute this, including commercially. If you
run a modified version as a network service, you must offer its users the
corresponding source of your version under the same licence, and you must keep
the attribution notices intact.


